[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding off on the edit — this one isn't confined to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is the shared client, and both scripts import it:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly production report export for finance)

  Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on the two [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls in [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m turns off certificate validation
  for the production export too, making that nightly job silently accept any certificate — a MITM on that path could
  feed or harvest finance data undetected. The self-signed staging cert is a real problem, but the fix as written lands
  outside staging.

  Options, if you want one:

  1. [1mScope it to staging[0m — make [38;5;153mverify[39m configurable in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m (e.g. a [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m env var or a module-level
